---
layout: article
title: Projects
description: Provision Appwrite projects and manage project API keys with Terraform, including the organization credentials they require.
---

Most provider resources live **inside** a project. These two sit above it: `appwrite_project` creates the project itself within an organization, and `appwrite_project_key` manages a project's API keys.

For full generated schemas, see the Terraform Registry: [project](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/appwrite/appwrite/latest/docs/resources/project) and [project_key](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/appwrite/appwrite/latest/docs/resources/project_key).

# Resources {% #resources %}

| Resource | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `appwrite_project` | Create a project within an organization |
| `appwrite_project_key` | Manage a project API key's name, scopes, and expiry |

{% info title="These resources need an organization API key" %}
Both are **organization-scoped**, so they authenticate with `organization_api_key` (or `APPWRITE_ORGANIZATION_API_KEY`) rather than the project `api_key`. Projects need `projects.read` and `projects.write`; keys need `keys.read` and `keys.write`. See [Configuration](/docs/tooling/terraform/provider#organization-credentials).
{% /info %}

# Creating a project {% #creating-a-project %}

```hcl
provider "appwrite" {
  endpoint             = "https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"
  organization_id      = "organization-id"
  organization_api_key = var.appwrite_organization_api_key
}

resource "appwrite_project" "example" {
  id     = "example-project"
  name   = "Example Project"
  region = "fra"
}
```

`region` defaults to the server's configured region; see [Regions](/docs/products/network/regions) for the available values. `organization_id` falls back to the provider-level value, and no provider-level `project_id` is required for this resource. Read-only attributes report `status`, `team_id`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`.

{% info title="Destroying a project destroys its data" %}
Removing an `appwrite_project` from your configuration deletes the project **and every resource and row inside it**. Consider a [`prevent_destroy` lifecycle block](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/meta-arguments/lifecycle) on projects that hold real data.
{% /info %}

You can wire a newly created project into the rest of your configuration by passing its ID to the resources that live inside it:

```hcl
resource "appwrite_tablesdb" "main" {
  project_id = appwrite_project.example.id
  name       = "main"
}
```

# Project API keys {% #project-api-keys %}

{% info title="Keys cannot be created by Terraform" %}
Appwrite **removed the endpoint for creating project API keys**, so an API key can no longer mint further keys and outlive the revocation of a leaked one. A plan that would create a key fails with guidance to create it in the Console instead. Create the key in the [Appwrite Console](https://cloud.appwrite.io), `terraform import` it, and Terraform manages its name, scopes, and expiry from then on. Read, update, delete, and import are unaffected.
{% /info %}

```hcl
resource "appwrite_project_key" "deployments" {
  id              = "deployments"
  project_id      = "project-id"
  organization_id = "organization-id"
  name            = "Deployment automation"
  scopes          = ["functions.read", "functions.write", "sites.read", "sites.write"]
  expire          = "2027-01-01T00:00:00.000+00:00"
}
```

`scopes` accepts up to 100 entries, and `expire` is an ISO 8601 timestamp — omit it for a key that does not expire. Read-only attributes report `accessed_at`, `sdks`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`.

{% info title="The secret is only ever returned at creation" %}
`secret` is **empty on an imported key**, because Appwrite only returns a secret when a key is created. Read it from the Console instead of from state. Where a `secret` is populated — on keys created before the endpoint was removed — it is written to Terraform state, so use an encrypted remote backend with restricted access.
{% /info %}

# Importing {% #importing %}

Identifiers are composed from the organization and project IDs, and the leading segments can be dropped when the provider already configures them:

```bash
terraform import appwrite_project.example <organization-id>/<project-id>
terraform import appwrite_project.example <project-id>

terraform import appwrite_project_key.deployments <organization-id>/<project-id>/<key-id>
terraform import appwrite_project_key.deployments <project-id>/<key-id>
terraform import appwrite_project_key.deployments <key-id>
```

On Terraform 1.5.0 and later you can use an import block instead, which is the more convenient form for a key you must import before it can be managed:

```hcl
import {
  to = appwrite_project_key.deployments
  id = "organization-id/project-id/deployments"
}
```

